.TH HELIOS-UNIX 7 "" "helios-unix" "Programming overview"
.SH NAME
helios-unix \- Unix compatibility interfaces for native Helios applications
.SH DESCRIPTION
helios-unix is an opt-in library using the native Helios ABI and resident
libraries. It provides selected facilities for porting Unix applications to
Transputers, including tasks running on processors without shared memory.
It does not provide Unix fork, pthreads, shared memory or a complete POSIX API.
.SH BUILDING AN APPLICATION
Use a Helios-NG 1.31-SP2 SDK with resident Posix version 1001, its matching
hu-spawn.h, llvm-t800 and the Helios assembler.
Use the generated SDK header directories in this order:
.IP 1. 3
include/helios-unix/compat (opt-in compatibility declarations).
.IP 2.
include/helios-unix (the helios/unix headers).
.IP 3.
The native SDK include directory.
.PP
Link helios-unix.lib before bsd.lib, c.lib and helios.lib.
The staged lib/helios-unix-flags.json records compiler definitions and include
order. Compile dependencies with the same header profile as the application.
Do not combine this archive with an unrelated libc ABI.
.SH CHOOSING AN INTERFACE
.TP
hu_spawn(3)
Start a separate task with arguments, environment, descriptor actions and
optional processor selection. Use waitpid and kill for its lifecycle.
.TP
hu_channel(3)
Create a bounded stream between tasks through the native FIFO server,
including tasks on different processors. No TCP stack is involved.
.TP
hu_poll(3)
Wait for sockets, native FIFO channels and regular files. Terminals and
native anonymous pipes are outside this API's supported object classes.
.TP
getaddrinfo(3)
Resolve IPv4 names synchronously. hu_resolve(3) runs lookup in a helper task
when the application must remain responsive.
.TP
hu_terminal(3)
Read dimensions, save/restore raw mode and optionally normalize native
console input. TERM alone does not describe the terminal service's key format.
.TP
hu_tls(3)
Use an owned TLS object independently of where encryption runs.
hu_tls_fd(3) adapts older descriptor/select event loops.
.TP
glob(3), mkstemp(3)
Expand filename patterns and create exclusive temporary files.
.SH ERROR HANDLING AND OWNERSHIP
Check each page's return convention: spawning returns an error number;
name lookup returns an EAI code; most other calls return -1 and set errno.
TLS handshake and shutdown steps distinguish pending from complete.
.PP
Close unused channel endpoints, reap helper tasks and release owned results.
Task descriptors do not share cached status or file positions in the Unix
fork sense. These interfaces are for the application thread or a single event
loop; they do not promise concurrent spawn/close or concurrent TLS-object use.
.SH RUNTIME REQUIREMENTS
Channels require /fifo with readiness support. Async lookup requires
/helios/lib/hu-resolve. DNS configuration is /helios/etc/resolv.cnf, with local
entries in /helios/etc/hosts.
.PP
The supplied TLS provider uses the Linux I/O server's optional TLS service
(IOS_TLS=1). The provider interface also permits a native byte-transport
engine, but this library does not include a Transputer cryptographic engine.
An unavailable provider fails explicitly; there is no plaintext fallback.
.SH SEE ALSO
hu_spawn(3), hu_channel(3), hu_poll(3), hu_resolve(3), hu_terminal(3), hu_tls(3)
